# What stays out of the .vsix.
#
# Default-deny would be better but vsce has no such mode, so this is a deny list and it has
# to be read as one: anything added to this package ships unless a line here stops it.
#
# The shipped extension is the bundle, the manifest, the icons and the README — nothing
# else. `bun build` inlines every import, so there is no runtime dependency to carry and
# `--no-dependencies` in the package script says so.

# Sources and their tests. The bundle is what runs; shipping the inputs too would double the
# download and invite someone to edit a file that is never loaded.
#
# `test/**` is here because it shipped once: `src/**` was denied and the tests were added
# later, straight past a rule that named the other directory. That is this file's failure
# mode in one line — a new directory ships by default and only the packing manifest says so.
src/**
test/**
tsconfig.json
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# Declaration output. `bun build` writes the bundle here and `tsc --build` writes types
# beside it; only the bundle belongs in the package.
dist/**/*.d.ts
dist/**/*.d.ts.map
dist/**/*.map

# Previously-built packages. A .vsix inside a .vsix is a recursion nobody wants.
dist/**/*.vsix

# Dev-only dependencies. @types/vscode and vsce are both build-time; with --no-dependencies
# this should already be empty, and this line makes that a guarantee rather than a hope.
node_modules/**

# Repo furniture that means nothing to an installed extension.
.vscodeignore
resources/icon.svg
